  "featured_image_url": "https://thedrakelawfirm.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/DRL-FAQ.png",
  "content": {
    "rendered": "<div class=\"ast-oembed-container \" style=\"height: 100%;\"><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Life After Divorce: What You Need to Know\" width=\"500\" height=\"281\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Bf1qNKwGURA?start=12&#038;feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Divorce marks the end of one chapter, but remember—it’s also the start of a new one. It’s okay to feel a mix of emotions—relief, fear, uncertainty, even hope. The question is, where do you go from here?</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">First, take a moment to pause. It’s important to allow yourself time to process everything emotionally and mentally. Consider reaching out to a therapist—it can really help clarify emotions and give you tools to move forward healthily.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">If kids are involved, a child therapist can be a lifeline in helping them adjust. One of the most crucial steps post-divorce is reevaluating your finances. Working with a financial planner is a great way to get a clear picture of your current situation and create a new financial roadmap.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Assess everything—from your budget and savings to retirement plans. If you’re unsure where to start, we’re happy to recommend trusted financial planners who can make the process clearer for you. Next, look at where you’ll call home. Whether you&#8217;re staying in your current house or looking for a fresh start, this can be a good time to work with a trusted realtor and lender to explore your options. Maybe it’s downsizing to something more manageable, finding a new neighborhood, or even starting fresh in a new city. Real estate professionals can help ensure you’re making a choice that fits your new life.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Lastly, remember that rebuilding your social and support networks takes time, but it’s worth the effort. Reconnect with family, nurture friendships, or explore hobbies and activities that bring you joy.</p>\n"
